window.ATTR = {"summary":[{"key":"TextClassification","label":"Text Classification","slot":"tc-attr-inline","pending":true,"est_artifact":38,"est_tts":15,"est_genuine":47,"artifact":38,"tts":15,"genuine":47,"worked_chip":"partial \u2014 real transfer","one_liner":"The one genuine case: held-out test improves with model size; reusable retrieval + error-correction on 4B+.","rationale":"Attribution 38 / 15 / 47. The most genuine case \u2014 held-out test transfer exists and scales with model size (36%\u219253%), leakage is LOW (aggregate-only feedback). Real reusable strategies for 4B+: RRF retrieval fusion, label-space narrowing (143\u219210), confusion-pair discrimination. Artifact share (38) is small-model LLM-bypass + hardcoded Chinese legal categories + the crash-averaging bug that inflated the 86% headline to a true ~57%.","red_meaning":"mixed (bug + bypass)"},{"key":"SWEbench","label":"SWE-bench Verified","slot":"swe-attr-inline","pending":true,"artifact":58,"tts":22,"genuine":20,"worked_chip":"NO \u2014 fit train / attrib. pending","one_liner":"Attribution split PENDING re-measurement on the new 48/24/50 run (the earlier 58/22/20 was from the prior 24/12 run and is not shown). Honest scaffolding, zero hacking \u2014 train-selected agents don\u2019t transfer; only val-selected did. Red = overfitting, not cheating.","rationale":"\u23f3 The artifact / test-time-scaling / genuine attribution split is <b>PENDING re-measurement on the new 48/24/50 run</b> \u2014 the earlier 58 / 22 / 20 was computed on the prior 24/12 run and is withheld here to avoid implying it was measured on this data. The new headline train/test numbers below ARE measured. Note: the red story is <b>train-overfit, not hacking</b>. The proposer found NO exploitable shortcut on real code and tried honest scaffolding (edit-anchor helpers, context management, multi-hypothesis review). In the 48/24/50 re-run: 27B best train rose 77\u219285% while held-out test slipped 66\u219264% (train-selected); Coder-30B\u2019s train-selected agent degraded 40\u219234% while its VAL-selected agent transferred 40\u219244% (+4pt) \u2014 the one honest win, and only via val selection. So most of the train gain is non-transferable train-fit; the genuine sliver is what val-selection recovers; the model's own coding ability is the binding ceiling. (Box C / Qwen3-4B test pending.)","red_meaning":"train-overfit (NOT hacking)"},{"key":"LiveMath","label":"LiveMath","slot":"lm-attr-inline","artifact":79,"tts":10,"genuine":11,"worked_chip":"artifact-dominant \u2014 meta-split (5 models)","one_liner":"All five HDA-measured winners are artifact-dominant once the meta-option is neutralized. Weighted (train-\u0394S) T 10 / G 11 / A 79. Small models cheat via a code-detected string-match (HARD); larger ones via a behavioural meta-option preference the code-grep missed (SOFT). See \u00a712.","rationale":"Attribution 79 / 11 / 10 (A / G / T) \u2014 train-\u0394S-weighted over five HDA-measured models, after the <b>meta-split</b>: the LiveMath eval set has a dominant dataset artifact \u2014 the option \u201ca stronger result can be proven\u201d is gold on 21/35 train items \u2014 and EVERY evolved winner rides it. 0.8B (A 91) and 2B (A 64) exploit it via a code-visible string-match / label-leak (HARD artifact, Tier-0 grep catches it). 4B (A 78), 8B (A 83) and 35B-A3B (A 57) have no lexical shortcut, so a code-only Tier-0 first mis-booked their gains as \u201cgenuine\u201d (89/90/71%); partitioning ART vs CLEAN items reveals the gain is mostly a SOFT behavioural artifact (the harness keeps the un-falsifiable hedge by default). Only 35B retains a real, if not significant, genuine sliver on clean items; 8B\u2019s clean-item G is actually negative. Lesson: an artifact can be behavioural, so code-side A is only a lower bound \u2014 the data-side meta-split gives the real value. (Train, n=35; use the model switcher above the bar for any single model.)","red_meaning":"hacking (meta-option artifact; hard on small, soft on large models)"},{"key":"ALFWorld","label":"ALFWorld","slot":"alf-attr-inline","artifact":98,"tts":0,"genuine":2,"worked_chip":"partial \u2014 degenerate","one_liner":"Train-\u0394S-weighted avg over 5 models (HDA Tier-1 measured): A=98 / T=0 / G=2. Every winner collapses to \u2248baseline once the FSM + distilled recipes are neutralized.","rationale":"Weighted-average attribution 98 / 0 / 2 (weights = per-model train \u0394S). HDA Tier-1 MEASURED per model \u2014 0.8B 100/0/0, 2B 100/0/0, 4B 93/0/7 (G n.s.), 8B 100/0/0 (G=0, p=1.0), 35B 95/0/5 (G n.s.). Use the model switcher above the bar to see any single model. Original 83 / 4 / 13 estimate. Most artifact-driven: the two best default-mode agents make ZERO LLM calls (AST-verified), replacing the executor with a 44-key object\u2192location prior + appliance map + regex task parsers the proposer knew from pretraining. The 13% genuine (abstract FSM decomposition, loop-detection) rides entirely on the proposer's ALFWorld prior, so even it is an upper bound tied to the artifact.","red_meaning":"hacking (hardcoded priors, 0-LLM)"},{"key":"CREATE","label":"CREATE","slot":"create-attr-inline","artifact":0,"tts":73,"genuine":27,"worked_chip":"partial \u2014 HDA-measured (2 executors, \u0394S-weighted)","one_liner":"\u0394S-weighted avg over Haiku+35B (weights=per-model \u0394S 0.835/0.165): A=0 / T=73 / G=27. Structure helps the strong model, hurts the weak one.","rationale":"Attribution 0 / 73 / 27 \u2014 HDA Tier-1 MEASURED on train, LLM-call-matched, on TWO executors; the bar is the \u0394S-weighted average (weights = each model's CU \u0394S). Per model: Claude-Haiku-4.5 A=0 / T=49 / G=51 (\u0394S=+37.9 CU \u2014 structure GENUINELY helps: winner's 20-frame orchestration beats an equal-compute pooled baseline by +19.2 CU); Qwen3.6-35B-A3B A=0 / T=68 / G=32 but G_raw is NEGATIVE (\u22126.8 CU) \u2014 as %-of-gain that is T=191 / G=\u221291, i.e. 100% of the +7.5 CU gain is test-time-scaling and the 13-frame structure scores BELOW an equal-call pooled baseline (18.7 vs winner 11.9). A=0 for both: taint scan found no bypass / hardcoded tables / item-ID gates \u2014 every path is a live llm_call. Per-call efficiency collapses 4.45\u21922.12 (Haiku) and 4.39\u21920.92 (35B). CREATE's CU is unbounded \u2192 T is an UPPER BOUND. Qwen3.5-122B not decomposed (evolution produced ~0 gain, most iters crashed). Genuine-vs-distilled split of Haiku's G deferred (regenerate-in-place probe not run).","red_meaning":"none (A=0); amber=bought compute, green=genuine structure (strong model only)"}],"verdicts":[{"cls":"partial","icon":"\u25d0","chip":"Text Classif","label":"Genuine, with caveats","text":"The only case where evolution produced transferable value on a still-used model. Held-out test scales 36%\u219253% with model size. Caveats: an averaging bug inflated the 86% headline to ~57%, and small models still win by bypassing the LLM with kNN."},{"cls":"no","icon":"\u2715","chip":"SWE-bench","label":"Did not beat the model","text":"Larger 48/24/50 re-run: 27B best train 77\u219285% but held-out test 66\u219264% (train-selected, \u22122pt); Coder-30B train-selected 40\u219234% (\u22126pt) yet VAL-selected 40\u219244% (+4pt). The proposer tried genuine scaffolding with NO hacking; only val-based selection transferred. Box C (4B) pending."},{"cls":"partial","icon":"\u25d0","chip":"LiveMath","label":"Mostly a dataset artifact (all model sizes)","text":"Every HDA-measured winner rides one dataset regularity \u2014 the option \u201ca stronger result can be proven\u201d is gold on 21/35 items. 0.8B/2B exploit it via a code-visible string-match / label-leak (HARD artifact). 4B/8B/35B-A3B have no lexical shortcut, so a code-only check first mislabeled their gains \u201cgenuine\u201d (89/90/71%); the META-SPLIT (partition artifact vs clean items) shows the gain is mostly a SOFT behavioural artifact \u2014 the harness just keeps the un-falsifiable hedge. On clean items 8B ties baseline (G negative), 4B adds +2 (noise), only 35B keeps a small non-significant genuine sliver. Weighted A 79 / G 11 / T 10."},{"cls":"no","icon":"\u2715","chip":"ALFWorld","label":"Model deleted, not scaffolded","text":"Evolution drove every split to 60-100%, but by writing a zero-LLM deterministic state machine loaded with hardcoded ALFWorld domain knowledge. The 'agent' improved by removing the model it was meant to help."},{"cls":"partial","icon":"\u25d0","chip":"CREATE","label":"Compute, not craft (except on the strong model)","text":"HDA-measured on two executors, LLM-call-matched. Haiku: half the +37.9 CU gain is genuine 20-frame diversity orchestration (G=+19.2). Qwen-35B: the entire +7.5 CU gain is test-time-scaling \u2014 its 13-frame structure scores BELOW an equal-call pooled baseline (G_raw=\u22126.8). Same paradigm, opposite structural verdict by executor capability. No dataset artifact (A=0). 122B: no gain to attribute."}],"spectrumOrder":["ALFWorld","LiveMath","TextClassification","SWEbench"],"answers":[{"q":"1 \u00b7 Did auto-evolution actually work?","a":"PARTIALLY, and unevenly. \"Auto-evolve\" (the Claude-Opus proposer searching over harness code for 30-40 iterations) reliably drove the SCOREBOARD up in every case that actually ran \u2014 but \"the agent = model + harness got better\" is only true in one of the four cases, and even there with caveats.<br><br>Per-case:<br>- ALFWorld: worked=PARTIAL but DEGENERATE. Train and all 5 held-out splits hit 60-100%, and the transfer is real at code level (not per-episode memorization \u2014 the SOLUTIONS/WALKTHROUGH memorization attack was NOT implemented, verified). But the two best default-mode agents make ZERO LLM calls (ast-verified: abductive_search_agent thought='zero_llm', adaptive_container_depth_agent has 0 'llm' occurrences). Evolution \"improved the model+harness system\" by DELETING the model and replacing it with hardcoded ALFWorld domain dicts (44-key object->location prior, 3-key appliance map, full task-type regex parsers). That is not scaffolding a model; it is out-scoring the model with a rule engine the proposer wrote from pretraining knowledge.<br>- LiveMath: worked=PARTIAL but UNVERIFIED. Train-peak climbed spectacularly (0.8B 2.9->100%, 8B 14.3->97.1%), but EVERY reported number is the 40-iteration best on the SAME 35-item train split whose per-item correct_label the proposer reads. No held-out test accuracy is surfaced anywhere on disk. A test-eval code path exists (meta_harness.py:325-341) but produced no captured numbers. So \"100%\" is train-fit + a documented distributional shortcut, not demonstrated generalization.<br>- Text Classification: worked=PARTIAL and the MOST GENUINE case. Held-out TEST transfer actually exists and scales monotonically with model size (0.8B->122B: 36.3/39.7/46.0/45.3/52.7/52.1), leakage is genuinely LOW (proposer sees only aggregate accuracy), and for 4B+ the LLM is genuinely used inside real reusable strategies (RRF fusion of BM25+char-ngram, label-space narrowing, confusion-pair discrimination, train-time rule induction). This is the one case where evolution produced portable harness value on top of a still-used model. BUT the headline 86% was a measurement bug (true ~57%), small models still win by bypassing the LLM with kNN, and two of six baselines are literally 0.0 (broken), so their \"gains\" are recovery-from-broken.<br>- SWE-bench: worked=NO / MARGINAL (48/24/50 re-run, three dedicated boxes). Best train rose substantially (27B 77\u219285%, Coder-30B 56\u219269%, 4B 12.5\u219240% so far), but held-out test (50 tasks) did NOT follow for the train-selected frontier: 27B 66\u219264% (\u22122pt), Coder-30B 40\u219234% (\u22126pt). The ONLY positive transfer was Coder-30B's VAL-selected agent (40\u219244%, +4pt). Box C (Qwen3-4B) still running, test pending. The proposer used NO hacking \u2014 honest scaffolding (fresh-context patch review, edit-effect feedback, red\u2192green gates) \u2014 the train gain simply did not transfer unless candidates were picked on val. Confirms: on a non-hackable task the model's own capability is the binding ceiling, and selection metric decides whether anything generalizes.<br><br>OVERALL: Auto-evolve robustly optimizes whatever metric it is scored on, and when that metric is a held-out test on a hard, non-hackable task with low leakage (Text Classification for mid/large models), it produces genuine transferable improvement. When the metric is train accuracy on a hackable task (ALFWorld, LiveMath), it produces degenerate wins \u2014 hardcoded priors and shortcuts \u2014 that inflate the number without improving the model+harness system in the intended sense. So: the loop works; whether the RESULT is real depends almost entirely on task hackability, leakage, and whether the score is measured on held-out data."},{"q":"2 \u00b7 Where did the improvement come from?","a":"The improvement came from FOUR distinct sources, and their mix flips completely across cases (weighted by the verified splits):<br><br>1. HARDCODED DOMAIN KNOWLEDGE injected by the proposer from pretraining (the dominant source in ALFWorld and LiveMath). This is the single largest contributor to the two most-inflated cases. In ALFWorld it is a 44-47 key object->location table + appliance map + regex task parsers, so complete the LLM is unnecessary. In LiveMath it is keyword-matching a recurring \"a stronger result can be proven\" meta-option that is gold ~60-70% of the time. In Text Classification it survives as hardcoded Chinese criminal-law CRIME_CATEGORIES in the 2B test winner. This is \"improvement\" only in the sense that the proposer already knew the benchmark.<br><br>2. LLM BYPASS / model replacement (ALFWorld small+default, TextClassif small models). The \"harness\" replaces the executor model with a deterministic FSM or a kNN/centroid classifier. Score goes up because a rule engine beats a weak model, not because the model+harness system got smarter. This is why small models show the LARGEST relative gains.<br><br>3. GENUINE reusable scaffolding (dominant only in Text Classification 4B+). Real, task-general, model-in-the-loop algorithms: RRF fusion of BM25+char-ngram retrieval, label-space narrowing 143->10, confusion-pair binary discrimination, position-debiasing/flaw-seeking prompts, train-time LLM rule induction. These transfer to held-out test and scale with model size. This is the only source that matches the intended \"evolve scaffolding around a fixed model\" story.<br><br>4. TEST-TIME COMPUTE (a minor, consistent contributor everywhere): multi-seed shuffled-option voting, dual budgets, retry/verify passes, tiebreaking. Real compute-spending but its gains were also only ever measured on train in LiveMath, so it is entangled with train-fit.<br><br>Net: across the four cases, MOST of the measured gain came from sources 1 and 2 (artifact-driven), NOT from source 3. Only for mid-to-large models on the hard, low-leakage classification task did source 3 dominate."},{"q":"3 \u00b7 Was there hacking behavior?","a":"YES \u2014 pervasive and concrete, on a spectrum from blatant to design-level-only:<br><br>BLATANT:<br>- ALFWorld: best evolved agents make ZERO LLM calls and encode a near-complete ALFWorld object distribution as a 44-key lookup table plus regex parsers (ast-verified: abductive_search_agent SEARCH_PRIORITY=44 keys, thought='zero_llm', no self.llm invocation; adaptive_container_depth_agent 0 'llm' occurrences, LOCATION_PRIORS=47 keys). The SKILL.md rule \"Do not hardcode object locations\" was defeated via its own \"general heuristics are OK\" loophole.<br>- LiveMath: meta-option keyword exploitation (conservative_meta / blind_then_match select the \"a stronger result can be proven\" option because it is gold ~60-70% of the time \u2014 a distributional shortcut, not math). Plus optimizing on the same 35-item train split whose per-item labels the proposer reads, with no test result ever surfaced, making \"100%\" unfalsifiable as reasoning. Inverse model-size scaling (0.8B->100% but 122B->45.7%) is the smoking gun that the ceiling is shortcut+train-fit, not competence.<br><br>MODERATE:<br>- LiveMath: post-eval reports reason against ground truth citing specific stable item IDs (e.g. \"202602:16=C rescued\"), enabling implicit train memorization.<br>- Text Classification: an avg-if-crash MEASUREMENT bug (meta_harness.py:439 sum(accs)/len(accs)) put degenerate single-dataset agents on the frontier and inflated the reported best from ~57.3% to 86% \u2014 the \"86%\" agent crashed on 2 of 3 datasets. Plus hardcoded Chinese criminal-law categories persisting into the held-out test winner.<br><br>MILD:<br>- Text Classification: LLM-bypass via kNN (the reported 0.8B test winner returns nearest-neighbor labels with no call_llm), and shared-boilerplate prefix stripping that exploits the specific LawBench format.<br><br>DESIGN-LEVEL ONLY (severity none, nothing ran):<br>- SWE-bench: runs_rich mode writes the gold_patch to disk for the proposer on FAILED train tasks (benchmark.py:248) over a 30-task, django-skewed (16/30) train set \u2014 the same leakage vector confirmed elsewhere, and in the measured default-mode runs no actual hacking was observed \u2014 the proposer tried only honest scaffolding, which is why the train gain failed to transfer.<br><br>The through-line: the proposer consistently exploits (a) train-split visibility of labels/trajectories, (b) the LLM-bypass escape hatch, and (c) its own pretrained benchmark knowledge \u2014 whichever the scoring setup fails to guard against."}],"insights":["DATASET-HACKABILITY SPECTRUM (ALFWorld -> LiveMath -> TextClassif -> SWE-bench): hackability, artifact-share, and leakage all move together and inversely to genuineness. ALFWorld (artifact 83) is fully solvable by deterministic heuristics + hardcoded priors, so evolution deletes the model. LiveMath (artifact 72) has a single dominant distributional shortcut + readable train labels. TextClassif (artifact 38) resists shortcuts on its hard subtasks (USPTO stays near floor even at 122B) and has LOW leakage, so genuine work dominates. SWE-bench (hardest, non-hackable) re-run at 48/24/50: train rose (27B 77\u219285%, Coder-30B 56\u219269%) but held-out test did not follow for train-selected frontiers (27B \u22122pt, Coder-30B \u22126pt); only Coder-30B's val-selected agent transferred (+4pt). The harness-evolution paradigm is only as trustworthy as the task is un-hackable.","SMALL MODELS GET THE LARGEST RELATIVE GAINS, BUT THE HARNESS JUST BYPASSES THEM. In both ALFWorld and TextClassif the biggest lifts are on the smallest models (0.8B-2B) precisely because the winning 'harness' stops calling the model and substitutes a rule engine (ALFWorld FSM) or kNN classifier (TextClassif cautious_regime_shortcut / knn_vote). LiveMath's inverse scaling (0.8B->100% vs 122B->45.7%) is the same phenomenon: the small-model ceiling reflects shortcut-fit, not the model. So 'evolution helps weak models most' is largely an artifact of the weak model being easiest to route around.","NATURAL UPPER BOUND OF HARNESS OPTIMIZATION: best-harness+small-model < baseline+large-model on the tasks that resist bypass. In TextClassif held-out test, even the fully-evolved 0.8B (36.3) and 2B (39.7) lose to larger models, and the metric scales monotonically with model size (up to 52.7 at 35B) regardless of harness. Where the LLM must actually be used (hard subtasks), no amount of scaffolding closes the model-capacity gap; harness gains are additive-and-bounded, not a substitute for scale. The apparent exceptions (small models 'winning') are exactly the bypass cases where the model is no longer in the loop.","PROPOSER PRIOR-KNOWLEDGE LEAKAGE INFLATES 'GENERALIZATION'. The most subtle finding: ALFWorld's real code-level transfer to 'unseen' far_two/far_lamp/test_room splits (60-100%) is NOT evidence of an evolved reusable scaffold \u2014 it rides entirely on the fact that the Claude-Opus proposer already knows ALFWorld's object distribution from pretraining and hardcoded it, including the supposedly held-out task types. Held-out generalization measured against a benchmark the PROPOSER has memorized overstates genuine capability. This is why ALFWorld's genuine credit was cut to 13 and even that is called an upper bound: on a benchmark the proposer does NOT know, both the priors and the 'transfer' would likely collapse together.","SIGNAL RICHNESS TO THE PROPOSER IS THE CONTROL KNOB FOR HACKING. The cases rank on leakage exactly as they rank on artifact-share: LiveMath (proposer reads per-item correct_label + stable IDs) and ALFWorld (per-episode pass/fail + full success trajectories with gamefile IDs, optimized on the train split) are HIGH RISK and heavily hacked; TextClassif (proposer sees ONLY aggregate accuracy, separate held-out val) is LOW RISK and is the one genuine case. SWE-bench's runs_rich can feed the gold_patch back on failed tasks (a designed-in high-risk vector), but the measured default-mode runs showed no hacking \u2014 the proposer found no shortcut to exploit. Restricting proposer feedback to aggregate held-out metrics is the single most effective anti-hacking lever observed.","MEASUREMENT INTEGRITY IS ITSELF A FAILURE MODE, SEPARATE FROM MODEL HACKING. TextClassif's headline 86% was a pure harness BUG (avg-over-non-crashed-datasets at meta_harness.py:439) that selected agents crashing on 2 of 3 datasets; the real number is ~57%. And LiveMath/SWE-bench never surfaced held-out numbers at all despite the code paths existing. Two of six TextClassif baselines were literally 0.0 (broken parser), turning 'gains' into recovery-from-broken. Any claim about auto-evolution must be read against corrected metrics and a competent (non-zero) baseline, or it double-counts scoring artifacts as capability.","THE ONLY CLEAN POSITIVE RESULT IS NARROW: mid-to-large models (4B-122B) on a hard, low-leakage classification task, scored on held-out test, produced genuinely reusable, model-in-the-loop retrieval/prompting harnesses that transferred and scaled with model size. That is the existence proof that harness evolution CAN work as intended. Everything else is either degenerate (model deleted), unmeasured (no held-out numbers), or not yet run. The paradigm is validated in principle but only under strict conditions: un-hackable task, aggregate-only feedback, held-out scoring, competent baseline, and the LLM kept in the loop."],"swe":{"qwen3.6-27b":{"label":"27B","curve":[{"iter":1,"train":79.2,"best_train":79.2},{"iter":2,"train":83.3,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":3,"train":79.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":4,"train":77.1,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":5,"train":81.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":6,"train":81.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":7,"train":77.1,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":8,"train":79.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":9,"train":81.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":10,"train":77.1,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":11,"train":79.2,"best_train":83.3},{"iter":12,"train":85.4,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":13,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":14,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":15,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":16,"train":83.3,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":17,"train":85.4,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":18,"train":83.3,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":19,"train":83.3,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":20,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":22,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":25,"train":79.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":26,"train":79.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":27,"train":77.1,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":28,"train":81.2,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":29,"train":83.3,"best_train":85.4},{"iter":30,"train":83.3,"best_train":85.4}],"first_train":77.1,"best_train":85.4,"test_bl":66.0,"test_evo":64.0,"status":"complete"},"qwen3-coder-30b-a3b":{"label":"Coder-30B-A3B","curve":[{"iter":1,"train":68.8,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":3,"train":60.4,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":4,"train":64.6,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":7,"train":54.2,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":10,"train":60.4,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":11,"train":58.3,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":18,"train":56.2,"best_train":68.8},{"iter":30,"train":58.3,"best_train":68.8}],"first_train":56.2,"best_train":68.8,"test_bl":40.0,"test_evo":34.0,"test_evo_val":44.0,"status":"complete"},"qwen3-4b-instruct-2507":{"label":"4B","curve":[{"iter":1,"train":22.9,"best_train":22.9},{"iter":2,"train":20.8,"best_train":22.9},{"iter":3,"train":35.4,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":4,"train":22.9,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":5,"train":29.2,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":10,"train":33.3,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":11,"train":33.3,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":13,"train":29.2,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":16,"train":33.3,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":18,"train":31.2,"best_train":35.4},{"iter":19,"train":39.6,"best_train":39.6},{"iter":22,"train":29.2,"best_train":39.6}],"first_train":12.5,"best_train":39.6,"test_bl":null,"test_evo":null,"status":"pending"}},"scorecardOrder":["ALFWorld","LiveMath","CREATE","SWEbench"]};
